00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.050 align:start position:0%
water has many amazing properties its  surface tension enables things like bugs  to walk across it without sinking water  also becomes less dense as it freezes  which is why during winter lakes freeze  from the top down and life persists at  the bottom now scientists have observed  a bizarre quantum mechanical trick in  the smallest possible droplet of water  waters ability to form hydrogen bonds is  behind most of its extraordinary feats  water molecules are always bonding with  one another switching partners like  dancers in order to study these bonds  researchers performed low-temperature  experiments on a cluster containing only  six molecules the smallest droplet that  will form a 3d shape by distinguishing  the individual water molecules in the  cluster they observed a surprising  result two different molecules in the  cluster move in sync breaking two  different hydrogen bonds at the same  time the rotating molecules mesh like  gears in a watch here's where the  weirdness of quantum mechanics comes in  at the low temperatures used in this  experiment the molecules don't jiggle  with enough energy to even break one  hydrogen bond there is an energy barrier  between the two configurations that's  too high to scale but thanks to quantum  uncertainty there's still a chance that  the cluster will simply pop through that  energy barrier a bit like walking  through a wall that weird trick is known  as quantum tunneling this unexpected cog  like motion in the tiniest droplet shows  once again how plain old water continues  to serve up scientific surprises
 

